{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact NF-3 head 3bb7327ccbce0a289f34998ccc4c1fb1120a2b97 on builder/client-custody-nf3-20261112b, all 35 artifacts, for request dd0d078a under promise 50178a3f and review request fd2239fb. The head does what dd0d078a asks. Client owns `Custody`, `CustodyStore`, the record types, `decisive` and `memoryStore`, and its source-only Node entry owns the file store and its locks. MCP keeps the SQLite store and imports the rest. The stored record bytes do not change. `#stoppedEarly` reads its error name through `dataOf`, as ruling 370a3732 moved into this head.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- `packages/client/src/custody.ts` is the MCP file moved with three changes. It drops the import of MCP's metrics. It adds `CustodyMetrics`, an optional third constructor argument. It calls that port at the same three sites after each write, inside a catch. MCP passes `custodyMetrics`, an object over its existing `custodyTransition` and `custodyStepTransition`. The only other constructor, `examples/repo-in-ai/review-control.ts:58`, passes no port. Before this head, its calls reached MCP's metrics with no active operation, so they emitted nothing then either.\n- `packages/client/src/node.ts` holds the lines that left `packages/mcp/src/node.ts`, apart from its header comment and one `randomUUID` import. MCP's Node entry imports `fileStore` from `@generalbusiness/dap-client/node` and re-exports the seven named file-store symbols. The MCP root re-exports 16 values and 12 types by name, as the note lists. No relative import of a custody file remains in MCP.\n- The browser SDK stays clean. `tsconfig.json` excludes `src/node.ts`, and `tsconfig.node.json` compiles it alone with Node types. `public-release.json` excludes `./node` from the published exports and `src/node.ts` from the inputs. `outputClosure` refuses an emitted `dist/src/node.js` or `node.d.ts`. `publishManifest` refuses any other exclusion set. The published client exports stay `.` and `./application`.\n- The golden fixture is 2,351 bytes with SHA-256 `a734bc6b…dd5a`. It equals byte for byte `custody-record-v1.json.txt` in the predecessor capture at 490291a1, and that capture's memory and file records are equal. The Client witness compares raw memory and file records with the fixture and reads both again through a fresh `Custody` and a reopened file store. The MCP witness compares the ordered SQLite chunk bytes and a reopened SQLite store. The same scene checks that a throwing observer cannot undo a completed write.\n- `#stoppedEarly` (`service.ts:517`) now reads `dataOf(result as Failure).error`. `dataOf` returns `structuredContent`, so behaviour does not change.\n- `Kept` and `AttemptJournal` do not change.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main a76764a7 is an ancestor, and the pushed branch equals the head. The builder's worktree is at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 35 paths that the 35 artifacts cover, all at the head. The four deleted MCP paths are moves. The primary artifact is neither stale nor retired. `git merge-tree` against main is clean.\n- Cheap checks. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 8 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean.\n- Seals. Each of the six changed seals covers a change that the head explains: an import line in `hosted.ts`, `hosted-retirement.ts` and `act-result.ts`, the named aliases in `index.ts`, and the imports, constructor and `#stoppedEarly` in `service.ts`. On my own clone of the head, `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 12 of 12, so the new values equal what the seal functions compute. `scripts/public-release-modules.test.mjs` passes 5 of 5. I ran no other tests.\n- Evidence. All 91 files that the manifest lists match their SHA-256 values in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence`. The gate at a91f1d6b wrote exit 0 and \"gate passed\", and its counts agree with END dd8122a3. Its tree equals the manifest's `checkpointTree`, and from a91f1d6b to the head only the note and the manifest change. The failed gates 8f490edc and 31fcce02 stay failed, and the note claims no pass for them.\n- CI. GitHub reports Card run 43353392611 and Page run 43353392617 as push events, attempt 1, completed and successful, at a91f1d6b.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Client owns one custody port and one record vocabulary. Node file access sits behind a separate entry that the browser build and the release guards both exclude. Metrics become a caller port, so Client no longer depends on MCP. The named aliases keep MCP importers working until NF-5, and the note states that limit.\n\nSecurity: no finding. Custody behaviour, lock order and stored bytes do not change, and the golden witnesses guard the bytes on all three stores. The release guards refuse publishing the Node entry. An observer exception cannot replace a completed write, and the head tests that case.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Three notes for a later head:\n\n1. `#stoppedEarly` gets its type from a cast, not from the result. If `CallResult` were a union discriminated on `isError`, the read would narrow with no cast.\n2. Two MCP tests import `../../client/test/support/custody.ts` across packages. NF-5 can move them or the scene builder to one owner.\n3. MCP's metric functions already catch their own exceptions, and Client now wraps each call in a second catch. Either catch alone would do.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing and deploys.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fd1c72025bed1758f793b524e64f63bd9cc3a9d3","binding":"assigned","head":"3bb7327ccbce0a289f34998ccc4c1fb1120a2b97","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:54028e8e7a54f6ce01d6535108ec07d28402cb7e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:50178a3f2c1aae865705f4bd329eda1a2bdf8882\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375